Designing Your Own Points System – Key Components & Considerations

To build a robust points system for your protocol, here are areas to carefully design:

1. Define Objectives

What user behaviours do you want to incentivize? (Trading volume, liquidity provision, content creation, referrals, holding, etc.)

What outcomes matter for your protocol? (TVL, active users, referrals, social reach, retention).

Make sure the points earned align with valuable actions for your protocol.

2. Activity Metrics & Weighting

Decide which actions count and how they map to points: e.g.,

1 point per US$ X of volume

Additional multiplier for open interest maintained

Bonus for first X trades to onboard users

Referral: 1 point per Y points of referred user

Content‑creation: points for posts/replies + extra for engagement

Set multipliers for higher‑value actions (e.g., deeper liquidity, less common tasks).

Consider non‑linear scoring: e.g., doubling volume doesn’t double points; more marginal utility for early activity. (Used by Lighter)

Keep formula partially opaque (to discourage farming) but publish general rules for transparency.

3. Point Supply & Distribution Schedule

Decide total points available (weekly/monthly limits) → creates scarcity and competitive incentive.

Set epochs/snapshots (e.g., weekly cycles, monthly phases) with cut‑offs.

Consider seasons/ phases: early heavy weighting for early adopters, then broader access later (Hyperliquid did this).

Communicate schedule: e.g. weekly pool resets, when points convert to tokens, deadlines to claim, etc.

4. Conversion to Token / Reward

Define how points translate to token allocation (or other reward):

E.g., “Your point share ÷ total points earned by all users” → your share of airdrop.

Or “points give rights to purchase token at certain price” (as USD.AI does).

Decide vesting or lock‑up rules for tokens earned (encourages long‑term alignment).

Clearly communicate what happens after points: claim process, deadlines, KYC if needed, wallet linking.

Ensure conversion mechanics are fair and understandable — even if exact formula is undisclosed, the rules must be clear.

5. Sybil / Abuse Mitigation

Identify possible abuse vectors: multiple accounts, wash trading, minimal meaningful actions aimed only at points.

Build in guards:

Minimum activity threshold (e.g., hold assets for N days, maintain open interest).

Monitor and penalise obvious wash trades, repeated open/close trades with no risk.

Multi‑account limits or linking social/wallet identities. (Kaito uses social login; Lighter has “max 10 accounts per user”).

Non‑linear rewards so that splitting activity across many wallets is less effective than concentrating.

Manual/algorithmic review of suspicious patterns.

Communicate: publicly declare you will disqualify obvious abuse to deter bad actors.

6. Gamification & Engagement

Use leaderboards (weekly/monthly) to drive competition and visibility.

Provide bonus campaigns or multipliers (early adopters, special tasks) to keep excitement high.

Enable referrals to get viral growth (but monitor for referral abuse).

Post‑airdrop reward continuity: continuing tasks, weekly rewards, loyalty incentives – to keep users engaged beyond initial event.

7. Transparency & Communication

Provide documentation: how points are earned (categories), distribution schedule, major rules, claim instructions.

Maintain updates: inform users of changes, epi­sodes, snapshots, bonus rounds.

Balance between transparency (trusted system) and opaqueness (prevent gaming).

After distribution, publish results: e.g., number of users, claim stats, tokenomics.

8. Inclusivity vs Exclusivity

Early phases often favour heavy users (large volume, big capital) but you might want later phases that let smaller users compete too (Hyperliquid’s shift from perpetuals to spot & holdings) 
Gate.com

Design task tiers: beginner tasks (low barrier) + advanced tasks (high barrier, bigger multiplier).

Consider wallet size/funding floor as eligibility criterion if you want a baseline.

9. Sustainability & Long‑Term Alignment

Avoid designing points solely as a giveaway; align earned tokens with long‑term value (stickiness, retention).

Provide ongoing rewards post‑TGE (staking, governance, community tasks) so users stay engaged not dump tokens.

Use portion of token supply for future community rewards/emissions (not only initial airdrop).

Summary

Both Hyperliquid and Kaito show that points systems work best when matched with real value‑driving actions, and when they strike a balance of scarcity, competition, and fairness.

Hyperliquid’s strength was trading volume + weekly fixed pools + evolving criteria.

Kaito’s strength was social‑engagement + quality weighting + identity linkage.

Your protocol can mix these elements: whichever user behaviour you want (trading, liquidity, content, referrals), design a points → reward path, fix a pool per epoch, provide multipliers for valuable actions, and incorporate anti‑abuse mechanisms.

Document clearly, communicate consistently, but keep the “exact formula” hidden (or evolve it) to prevent purely mechanical farming.

Plan for long‑term value so that users who earn points become aligned stakeholders, not just one‑time reward seekers.